    <statement><p>If
<me>z = \cos\mathopen{}\left(\frac{y}{x}\right),\qquad x = 2t,\qquad y = 5-t^{2},</me>
find <m>dz/dt</m> using the chain rule. Assume the variables are
restricted to domains on which the functions are defined.</p>
<p><m>\frac{dz}{dt} =</m> <fillin characters="65" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2\frac{\left(5-t^{2}\right)\sin\mathopen{}\left(\frac{5-t^{2}}{2t}\right)}{\left(2t\right)^{2}}+\frac{\sin\mathopen{}\left(\frac{5-t^{2}}{2t}\right)}{2t}\cdot 2t</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><me>\frac{dz}{dt} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt} +
\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}</me>
<me>= (\frac{y\sin\mathopen{}\left(\frac{y}{x}\right)}{x^{2}})(2) + (\frac{-\sin\mathopen{}\left(\frac{y}{x}\right)}{x})(-2t)</me>
<me>= 2\frac{\left(5-t^{2}\right)\sin\mathopen{}\left(\frac{5-t^{2}}{2t}\right)}{\left(2t\right)^{2}}+\frac{\sin\mathopen{}\left(\frac{5-t^{2}}{2t}\right)}{2t}\cdot 2t.</me>




</solution>
